[[:db/cas 17592186124887 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2}=1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124887 :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124889 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} + ((z + 1)^{2})/(4) = 1$?" "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} + ((z + 1)^{2})/(4) = 1\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186124891 :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$" "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle z+1=\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124892 :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1?$" "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124893 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=(z-4)^{2}\\,?$" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=(z-4)^{2}\\,?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124895 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ and $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}}$ be bases of $R^{2}$.\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$[b_{1}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, [b_{2}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$." "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ and $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}}$ be bases of $R^{2}$.\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle [b_{1}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, [b_{2}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124914 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n$$\n\\displaystyle {P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ."] [:db/cas 17592186124923 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}?$" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle z-3=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124931 :question/problem "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "Given the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle z+4=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4},\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186124956 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$" "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j\n$$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124968 :question/problem "$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ to the basis $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x = 5b_{1} - b_{2}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ to the basis $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x = 5b_{1} - b_{2}$."] [:db/cas 17592186124988 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125001 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xyz\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}}$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xyz)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xyz)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xyz| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xyz<1.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xyz\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}}\n$$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xyz)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xyz)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xyz| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xyz<1.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125002 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$"] [:db/cas 17592186125002 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186125011 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125012 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $λ$ of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$." "Determine all real eigenvalues $λ$ of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125013 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125016 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $λ$ of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$." "Determine all real eigenvalues $λ$ of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125017 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125031 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "Given the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125041 :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}, C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of $R^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle B = {\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}, C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of $R^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}$."] [:db/cas 17592186125042 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-4$, $λ_{2} =-2$, and $λ_{3} = 2$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D = P^{-1}AP$." "$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-4$, $λ_{2} =-2$, and $λ_{3} = 2$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D = P^{-1}AP$."] [:db/cas 17592186125044 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $λ_{1} = 3$, $λ_{2} =-3$, $λ_{3} =-6$ are eigenvalues, and\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a, b, c$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$?" "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $λ_{1} = 3$, $λ_{2} =-3$, $λ_{3} =-6$ are eigenvalues, and\n$$\n\\displaystyle v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a, b, c$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186125084 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-6y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y &=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6x &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=-6 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=-6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-(-6)^{2}=-36<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,2)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,2)=det({f}^{″}(2,2))=12\\cdot 12-(-6)^{2}=108>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(2,2)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,2)=({f}^{″}(2,2))_{11}=12>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-6y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y &=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6x &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=-6 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=-6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-(-6)^{2}=-36<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,2)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,2)=det({f}^{″}(2,2))=12\\cdot 12-(-6)^{2}=108>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(2,2)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,2)=({f}^{″}(2,2))_{11}=12>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125086 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ 24 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ 24 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125088 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125098 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ then ${A}^{-1}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186125103 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x - y \\\\ -8x + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$?" "Consider the linear transformation\n$$\n\\displaystyle T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x - y \\\\ -8x + 2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand the vectors\n$$\n\\displaystyle v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$?"] [:db/cas 17592186125106 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$" "Given the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125110 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, find $T(v)$.\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, find $T(v)$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125112 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $T$, where\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2z + 3y - x \\\\ 2y - 3z + x \\\\ z - x + 3y\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $T$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2z + 3y - x \\\\ 2y - 3z + x \\\\ z - x + 3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125118 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)<8$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)<8$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)<8$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)<8$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186125124 :question/problem "$[T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: V → W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x ∈ V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle [T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: V → W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x ∈ V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$."] [:db/cas 17592186125129 :question/problem "$[T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: V → W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x ∈ V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle [T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: V → W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x ∈ V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$."] [:db/cas 17592186125130 :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of $R^{3}$.\nThe matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: R^{2} → R^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x ∈ R^{2}$, are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of $R^{3}$.\nThe matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: R^{2} → R^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x ∈ R^{2}$, are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$."] [:db/cas 17592186125134 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-3y - 2z \\\\ x - 2z - 4y \\\\ z - 3x + 4y\\end{bmatrix}$, find $nullity(T)$." "Given the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-3y - 2z \\\\ x - 2z - 4y \\\\ z - 3x + 4y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $nullity(T)$."] [:db/cas 17592186125136 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x - 2y + 3z \\\\ 4x + y + z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $Ker(T)$?" "Consider the linear transformation\n$$\n\\displaystyle T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x - 2y + 3z \\\\ 4x + y + z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand the vectors\n$$\n\\displaystyle v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $Ker(T)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186125138 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y+2z \\\\ x-3y+z \\\\ 4x-4y+z\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" "Consider the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y+2z \\\\ x-3y+z \\\\ 4x-4y+z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125142 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of $R^{2}$ given by\n$C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$.\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the map $T: V → R^{2}$ relative to these bases if\n$T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$." "Consider the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of $R^{2}$ given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the map $T: V → R^{2}$ relative to these bases if\n$$\n\\displaystyle T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125144 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$ is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$ is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186125164 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$ is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$ is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186125216 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0.5,0.5)$ and $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0.5,0.5)$  satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.\n$$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0.5,0.5)$ and $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0.5,0.5)$  satisfy the inequality $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.\n$$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"] [:db/cas 17592186125217 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{9}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186125218 :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.\n$$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?"] [:db/cas 17592186125231 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of $R^{3}$. The matrix $[T]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ is given by\n\n$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$, given that\n\n$T(b_{1})|= b_{2} - b_{3},; T(b_{2})|= b_{1} - b_{2} - b_{3},; T(b_{3})|= b_{1} + b_{2}$." "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of $R^{3}$. The matrix $[T]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$, given that\n\n$T(b_{1})|= b_{2} - b_{3},; T(b_{2})|= b_{1} - b_{2} - b_{3},; T(b_{3})|= b_{1} + b_{2}$."] [:db/cas 17592186125232 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $T$, where\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y - x \\\\ y + x\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $T$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y - x \\\\ y + x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125238 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "Given the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186125719 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases." "How do we calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?\n$$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases."] [:db/cas 17592186125728 :tutorial/content "Since the gradient of a function of several variables is made up of partial derivatives, we can expect it to have properties that are similar to partial derivatives.\n\nAssume that $f$ and $g$ are functions whose first-order partial derivatives exist and are continuous on some open domain, and that $a,b,$ and $c$ are real constants. Then, the gradient has the following properties:\n\n- Constant rule:  $\\nabla c = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- Linearity: $\\nabla (af + bg) = a \\nabla\\! f + b\\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Product Rule: $\\nabla(fg) = g \\nabla\\! f + f \\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Quotient Rule: $\\nabla \\left( \\dfrac{f}{g}\\right) = \\dfrac{ g \\nabla\\! f - f \\nabla\\! g}{g^2}$" "Since the gradient of a function of several variables is made up of partial derivatives, we can expect it to have properties that are similar to partial derivatives.\n\nAssume that $f$ and $g$ are functions whose first-order partial derivatives exist and are continuous on some open domain, and that $a,b,$ and $c$ are real constants. Then, the gradient has the following properties:\n\n- Constant rule:  $\\nabla c = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- Linearity: $\\nabla (af + bg) = a \\nabla\\! f + b\\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Product Rule: $\\nabla(fg) = g \\nabla\\! f + f \\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Quotient Rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla \\left( \\dfrac{f}{g}\\right) = \\dfrac{ g \\nabla\\! f - f \\nabla\\! g}{g^2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125743 :tutorial/content "To calculate the limit at infinity of a rational function, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{x^4}{x^5-x^2}\\right),\n$$\n\n we apply the following procedure:\n\n**Step 1:** Divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the *denominator*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}÷{x}^{5}}{({x}^{5}-{x}^{2})÷{x}^{5}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}}}{\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the rule that if $n$ is any positive integer then $\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)=0.$ Evaluate the remaining expression to obtain the answer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To calculate the limit at infinity of a rational function, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{x^4}{x^5-x^2}\\right),\n$$\n\n we apply the following procedure:\n\n**Step 1:** Divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the *denominator*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}÷{x}^{5}}{({x}^{5}-{x}^{2})÷{x}^{5}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}}}{\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the rule that if $n$ is any positive integer then $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)=0.\n$$ Evaluate the remaining expression to obtain the answer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125784 :tutorial/content "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is **invertible** if there is another linear transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}))=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(\\mathbf{v}))=\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\mathbb R^n.$ The transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is called the **inverse** of $\\mathbf T.$ An invertible linear transformation is also called a **bijection**. \n\nWe have the following theorem regarding invertible linear transformations: \n\n*If $T$ is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T$, then $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if $T$ is invertible*.\n\nRecall that the invertible matrix theorem in terms of dimension, rank, and nullity states that if $T$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, then \n\n$T$ is invertible $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$\n$$\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( T ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( T ) ) = n = \\text{rank} ( T ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( T ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( T ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( T ) .\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the theorem above, we can make the following swaps, where $\\mathbf T$ is a linear transformation with the standard matrix $T{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} )\\end{array}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the **invertible matrix theorem in terms of linear transformations:**\n\n $\\mathbf T$ is a bijection (invertible) $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible" "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is **invertible** if there is another linear transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}))=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(\\mathbf{v}))=\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\mathbb R^n.$ The transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is called the **inverse** of $\\mathbf T.$ An invertible linear transformation is also called a **bijection**. \n\nWe have the following theorem regarding invertible linear transformations: \n\n*If $T$ is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T$, then $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if $T$ is invertible*.\n\nRecall that the invertible matrix theorem in terms of dimension, rank, and nullity states that if $T$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, then \n\n$T$ is invertible $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$\n$$\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( T ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( T ) ) = n = \\text{rank} ( T ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( T ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( T ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( T ) .\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the theorem above, we can make the following swaps, where $\\mathbf T$ is a linear transformation with the standard matrix $T{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} )\\end{array}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the **invertible matrix theorem in terms of linear transformations:**\n\n $\\mathbf T$ is a bijection (invertible) $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186125797 :tutorial/content "What is the matrix of the linear transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 4 y \\\\ - x - y\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nrelative to the basis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}, {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}} \\right\\}\\:?\n$$\n\nIn other words, what is the matrix $[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = [T]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}?\n$$\n\nIn fact, the answer is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the images of the basis vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2$ under the transformation relative to the basis:\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\color{red}{[ T ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) ]}_{\\mathcal{B}}} & {\\color{blue}{[ T ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) ]}_{\\mathcal{B}}} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is called the **matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$** or the **$\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}.$**\n\nAs a result, for the transformation $\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 4 y \\\\ - x - y\\end{array} \\right]}$, with respect to the basis \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ {\\color{red} \\mathbf{b}_1} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}, {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{b}_2} = {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n$$\n{\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)} =\t{\\color{red} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}7 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} }\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) } = {\\color{blue} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}11 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]} }.\n$$\n\nNow, to write ${\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}$ and ${\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) }$ with respect to our basis $\\mathcal{B},$ we assume that \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}} \n\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_2\\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}},\n$$\nwhere the components $\\alpha_1,$ $\\alpha_2,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\alpha_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{red}\\alpha_2}\\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}\\quad \\text{and}\\quad {\\color{blue}\\beta_1}\\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}\\beta_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSolving these two systems gives\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}}= {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ - 9\\end{array} \\right]}} \n\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_2\\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} ={\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}= {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}25 \\\\ - 14\\end{array} \\right]}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}16} & {\\color{blue}25} \\\\ {\\color{red}- 9} & {\\color{blue}- 14}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" "What is the matrix of the linear transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 4 y \\\\ - x - y\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nrelative to the basis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}, {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}} \\right\\}\\:?\n$$\n\nIn other words, what is the matrix $[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = [T]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}?\n$$\n\nIn fact, the answer is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the images of the basis vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2$ under the transformation relative to the basis:\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\color{red}{[ T ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) ]}_{\\mathcal{B}}} & {\\color{blue}{[ T ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) ]}_{\\mathcal{B}}} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is called the **matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$** or the **$\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}.$**\n\nAs a result, for the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 4 y \\\\ - x - y\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ with respect to the basis \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ {\\color{red} \\mathbf{b}_1} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}, {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{b}_2} = {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n$$\n{\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)} =\t{\\color{red} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}7 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} }\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) } = {\\color{blue} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}11 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]} }.\n$$\n\nNow, to write ${\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}$ and ${\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) }$ with respect to our basis $\\mathcal{B},$ we assume that \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}} \n\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_2\\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}},\n$$\nwhere the components $\\alpha_1,$ $\\alpha_2,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\alpha_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{red}\\alpha_2}\\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}\\quad \\text{and}\\quad {\\color{blue}\\beta_1}\\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}\\beta_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSolving these two systems gives\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}}= {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ - 9\\end{array} \\right]}} \n\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_2\\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} ={\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}= {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}25 \\\\ - 14\\end{array} \\right]}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}16} & {\\color{blue}25} \\\\ {\\color{red}- 9} & {\\color{blue}- 14}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125798 :tutorial/content "In general, consider a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\dots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V$ and a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow V$. For any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+\\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{b}_n\n$$\n\nor, equivalently $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}$.\nThen the matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ is given by\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} =\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & \\cdots  & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{n} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & \\cdots  & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{n} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\large[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}}\n\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{[\\large\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}}.\n$$" "In general, consider a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\dots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V$ and a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow V$. For any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+\\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{b}_n\n$$\n\nor, equivalently $$\n\\displaystyle [\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nThen the matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ is given by\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} =\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & \\cdots  & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{n} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & \\cdots  & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{n} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\large[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}}\n\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{[\\large\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\\\\n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same." "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\\\\n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.\n$$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same."] [:db/cas 17592186125888 :tutorial/content "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ | ![](images/09/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/76/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/cb/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$ | ![](images/df/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1$ | ![](images/08/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/0f/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)\n$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$" "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1\n$$ | ![](images/09/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/76/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/cb/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$ | ![](images/df/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1\n$$ | ![](images/08/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}\n$$ | ![](images/0f/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)\n$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=0 \\\\ 2y-x &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=0 \\\\ 2y-x &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,\n$$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186128838 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 v \\cos   u \\\\ v \\sin   u\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 v \\cos   u \\\\ v \\sin   u\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128838 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]}  = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]}  = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128858 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.$*" "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186128867 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overline{x},$ of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+8+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2.\n$$" "The center of mass, denoted $\\overline{x},$ of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+8+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128868 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9+0+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9+0+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128871 :question/problem "What is the value of  $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} ?$" "What is the value of  $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} ?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128871 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}\\times (-2)^{-2}+\\frac{2}{3} &= \\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 4}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{4}\\times 1}{3\\times \\cancel{4}}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle (-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}\\times (-2)^{-2}+\\frac{2}{3} &= \\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 4}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{4}\\times 1}{3\\times \\cancel{4}}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128892 :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+{t}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+{t}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128898 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2\n$$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186128899 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1\n$$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186128900 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186128900 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,\n$$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."] [:db/cas 17592186128928 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\sqrt{x-2}$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\sqrt{x-2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128929 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\sqrt{3-x}$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\sqrt{3-x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128932 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -3} \\sqrt[3]{3x-2x^2}$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -3} \\sqrt[3]{3x-2x^2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128975 :question/problem "Find $B=\\overline{A},$ where $A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 \\text{i} & 3 - \\text{i} & 1 + \\text{i} \\\\ 2 - 2 \\text{i} & 2 - \\text{i} & 1 + 2 \\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & 3 \\text{i} & 2 \\text{i}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ What is the value of $b_{11} + b_{23}?$" "Find $B=\\overline{A},$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 \\text{i} & 3 - \\text{i} & 1 + \\text{i} \\\\ 2 - 2 \\text{i} & 2 - \\text{i} & 1 + 2 \\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & 3 \\text{i} & 2 \\text{i}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ What is the value of $b_{11} + b_{23}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186128983 :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4}  \\int_{0}^{2} \\dfrac{x^3}{y^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x  \\, \\textrm{d}y.$" "Evaluate the repeated integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4}  \\int_{0}^{2} \\dfrac{x^3}{y^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x  \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128984 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} \\dfrac y{x^2} \\, \\textrm d A$, where \n$D=\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:2 \\leq x \\leq 4,\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 3\\right\\}.$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} \\dfrac y{x^2} \\, \\textrm d A,\n$$ where \n$D=\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:2 \\leq x \\leq 4,\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 3\\right\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186128985 :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.$\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}y \\, \\text{d}x$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{2}  \\int_{0}^{1} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, \\text{d}y\\,  \\text{d}x$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}x \\, \\text{d}y$" "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}y \\, \\text{d}x\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{2}  \\int_{0}^{1} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, \\text{d}y\\,  \\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}x \\, \\text{d}y\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128989 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} xy\\ \\!e^{x+y}\\ \\textrm d A$ over the rectangle $R= [0,1] \\times [1,2].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} xy\\ \\!e^{x+y}\\ \\textrm d A\n$$ over the rectangle $R= [0,1] \\times [1,2].$"] [:db/cas 17592186128990 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} \\left(x^2-y^2 +3\\right)  \\textrm d A,$  where $R= [-1,3] \\times [2, 4].$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} \\left(x^2-y^2 +3\\right)  \\textrm d A,\n$$  where $R= [-1,3] \\times [2, 4].$"] [:db/cas 17592186128991 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}2 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ if} \\hspace{1em} 0 < x < 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} - 1 < y < 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x y + x + y + 1}} , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ if} \\hspace{1em} 0 < x < 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} 0 < y < 1 ,\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}2 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ if} \\hspace{1em} 0 < x < 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} - 1 < y < 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x y + x + y + 1}} , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ if} \\hspace{1em} 0 < x < 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} 0 < y < 1 ,\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [:db/cas 17592186128992 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^3\\sum_{i=1}^2 (i+j).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^3\\sum_{i=1}^2 (i+j).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128997 :question/worked-solution "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  x}} = 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  y}} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 0 \\\\ 2 y + x = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$ \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  x}} = 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  y}} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 0 \\\\ 2 y + x = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$ \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186129000 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <         2\\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d60214a4b32c4462d422372aa90e78944649811ce0b1e277f07fe90663e5be94.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are $\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right).$" "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <         2\\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d60214a4b32c4462d422372aa90e78944649811ce0b1e277f07fe90663e5be94.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are $\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186129003 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where $\\rho \\geq 0,$ $0 \\leq \\theta <   2\\pi,$ and $0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following: \n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right).$" "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where $\\rho \\geq 0,$ $0 \\leq \\theta <   2\\pi,$ and $0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following: \n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129008 :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1 } 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$" "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1 } 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129009 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" "Change the order of integration in the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129010 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$" "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129011 :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d180fd5de88ebc465df522879142e5a4c5446c758aaccde792092968078c254.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x=2y^3-2y$ and  $x=y^3-4y,$ and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm{d}A.$" "![](images/1d/1d180fd5de88ebc465df522879142e5a4c5446c758aaccde792092968078c254.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x=2y^3-2y$ and  $x=y^3-4y,$ and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129014 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint\\limits_{R} y \\cos{x} \\sin{z} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $R = \\bigg\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi \\bigg\\}.$" "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint\\limits_{R} y \\cos{x} \\sin{z} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $$\n\\displaystyle R = \\bigg\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi \\bigg\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129016 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129017 :question/problem "![](images/17/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "![](images/17/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [:db/cas 17592186129023 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x-1}$." "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129039 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\\, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute $\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = (x_m^2 - x_0^2)(y_n-y_0)\n$$" "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\\, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute $$\n\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = (x_m^2 - x_0^2)(y_n-y_0)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129046 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_n = -6a_{n-1} - 12a_{n-2}, \\quad a_0 =-1, \\quad a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$.\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n$a_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)$,\nfind the value of $A + B$." "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_n = -6a_{n-1} - 12a_{n-2}, \\quad a_0 =-1, \\quad a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$.\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right),\n$$\nfind the value of $A + B$."] [:db/cas 17592186129046 :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = -1$ into the general solution gives\n$(2\\sqrt{3})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|=-1; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|=-1; A|=-1$.\n- Substituting $a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$ and $A=-1$ into the general solution gives\n$(2\\sqrt{3})^{1}(-1 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 3 + \\sqrt{3}B|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; B|= 1$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA + B = -1 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (2\\sqrt{3})^{n}(\\sin \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = -1$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle (2\\sqrt{3})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|=-1; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|=-1; A|=-1.\n$$\n- Substituting $a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$ and $A=-1$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle (2\\sqrt{3})^{1}(-1 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 3 + \\sqrt{3}B|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; B|= 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA + B = -1 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (2\\sqrt{3})^{n}(\\sin \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129047 :question/worked-solution "Moving all terms of our recurrence relation to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\na_n - 2a_{n-1} + 2a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 2λ + 1) - 1 + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ - 1 &= ± \\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 1 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 1 + \\textrm{i}, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 1 - \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nExpressing our solutions in polar form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)), \\\\\nλ_{2} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nWe now find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions:\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$(\\sqrt{2})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 2; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|= 2; A|= 2$.\n- Substituting $a_1=5$ and $A=2$ into the general solution gives\n$(\\sqrt{2})^{1}(2 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 5; \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})|= 5; 2 + B|= 5; B|= 3$.\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$" "Moving all terms of our recurrence relation to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\na_n - 2a_{n-1} + 2a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 2λ + 1) - 1 + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ - 1 &= ± \\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 1 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 1 + \\textrm{i}, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 1 - \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nExpressing our solutions in polar form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)), \\\\\nλ_{2} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nWe now find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions:\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 2; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|= 2; A|= 2.\n$$\n- Substituting $a_1=5$ and $A=2$ into the general solution gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2})^{1}(2 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 5; \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})|= 5; 2 + B|= 5; B|= 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129060 :question/worked-solution "We have the Pythagorean triple\n\n$$\n(a,b,c) = (8,15,17)\n$$\n\nComparing our polynomial identity with\n\n$$\na^2+b^2 = c^2\n$$\n\nwe have two possible options:\n\n- Option 1: $\\quad x^2-y^2 = 8, \\qquad 2xy = 15, \\qquad x^2+y^2 = 17$\n- Option 2: $\\quad x^2-y^2 = 15, \\qquad 2xy = 8, \\qquad x^2+y^2 = 17$\n\nWe'll try both options to see which generates the required triple:\n\n- Considering the first option: Adding the first and third equations, we get\n$\\begin{array}{rl} x^2-\\cancel{y^2}&=8 \\\\ x^2+\\cancel{y^2}&=17 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (x^2+x^2) &= (8+17) \\end{array}$\nwhich gives\n$2x^2 = 25\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2 = \\dfrac{25}2$,\nand we can disregard this option since $\\dfrac{25}2$ is not a perfect square:\n- Considering the second option: Adding the first and third equations, we get\n$\\begin{array}{rl} x^2-\\cancel{y^2}&=15 \\\\ x^2+\\cancel{y^2}&=17 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (x^2+x^2) &= (15+17) \\end{array}$\nwhich gives\n$2x^2 = 32\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=4$.\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n$2\\cdot 4y = 8 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=1$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=p=4$ and $y=q=1$ generate the required triple. Let's just test to make sure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2}–y^{2})^{2} + (2xy)^{2} &= (x^{2} + y^{2})^{2} \\\\\n(4^{2}–1^{2})^{2} + (2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 1)^{2} &= (4^{2} + 1^{2})^{2} \\\\\n(16–1)^{2} + 8^{2} &= (16 + 1)^{2} \\\\\n15^{2} + 8^{2} &= 17^{2} \\\\\n8^{2} + 15^{2} &= 17^{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np+q = 4+1 = 5\n$$" "We have the Pythagorean triple\n\n$$\n(a,b,c) = (8,15,17)\n$$\n\nComparing our polynomial identity with\n\n$$\na^2+b^2 = c^2\n$$\n\nwe have two possible options:\n\n- Option 1: $\\quad x^2-y^2 = 8, \\qquad 2xy = 15, \\qquad x^2+y^2 = 17$\n- Option 2: $\\quad x^2-y^2 = 15, \\qquad 2xy = 8, \\qquad x^2+y^2 = 17$\n\nWe'll try both options to see which generates the required triple:\n\n- Considering the first option: Adding the first and third equations, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{rl} x^2-\\cancel{y^2}&=8 \\\\ x^2+\\cancel{y^2}&=17 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (x^2+x^2) &= (8+17) \\end{array}\n$$\nwhich gives\n$2x^2 = 25\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2 = \\dfrac{25}2$,\nand we can disregard this option since $\\dfrac{25}2$ is not a perfect square:\n- Considering the second option: Adding the first and third equations, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{rl} x^2-\\cancel{y^2}&=15 \\\\ x^2+\\cancel{y^2}&=17 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (x^2+x^2) &= (15+17) \\end{array}\n$$\nwhich gives\n$2x^2 = 32\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=4$.\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n$2\\cdot 4y = 8 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=1$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=p=4$ and $y=q=1$ generate the required triple. Let's just test to make sure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2}–y^{2})^{2} + (2xy)^{2} &= (x^{2} + y^{2})^{2} \\\\\n(4^{2}–1^{2})^{2} + (2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 1)^{2} &= (4^{2} + 1^{2})^{2} \\\\\n(16–1)^{2} + 8^{2} &= (16 + 1)^{2} \\\\\n15^{2} + 8^{2} &= 17^{2} \\\\\n8^{2} + 15^{2} &= 17^{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np+q = 4+1 = 5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129401 :question/problem "What is $18 \\div \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{-2} + (\\frac{1}{101} - 0.01)^{0}$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle 18 \\div \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{-2} + (\\frac{1}{101} - 0.01)^{0}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129405 :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} 