in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $y$, then $x$, and then $z$, express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{-z}^{x/3}f(x, y, z)dydzdx + \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\displaystyle \\int_{-z}^{x/3}f(x, y, z)dydzdx\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129522 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x^{2}/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{5x + 9}}^{3\\sqrt{x + 1}}ye^{4z - z^{2}}dydzdx$.\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $dydxdz$.*" "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x^{2}/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{5x + 9}}^{3\\sqrt{x + 1}}ye^{4z - z^{2}}dydzdx.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $dydxdz$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186129529 :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186129541 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =5, & \\,{m}_{3} & =7, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.5, & \\,{x}_{2} & =5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+5+7 \\\\  & =14\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1.5+5\\cdot 5+7\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =3+25+70 \\\\  & =98\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{98}{14}=7.\n$$" "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =5, & \\,{m}_{3} & =7, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.5, & \\,{x}_{2} & =5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+5+7 \\\\  & =14\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1.5+5\\cdot 5+7\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =3+25+70 \\\\  & =98\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{98}{14}=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129542 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =3, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, & \\,{m}_{4} & =5, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3, & \\,{x}_{3} & =5, & \\,{x}_{4} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5+5\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2+9+5+30 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{46}{11}.\n$$" "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =3, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, & \\,{m}_{4} & =5, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3, & \\,{x}_{3} & =5, & \\,{x}_{4} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5+5\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2+9+5+30 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{46}{11}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129543 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1,\\,{m}_{4}=5.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2-6+1-15 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=-\\frac{18}{11}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1,\\,{m}_{4}=5.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2-6+1-15 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=-\\frac{18}{11}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129577 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x) = 2\\cot (\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{π}{4})$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = 2\\cot (\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{π}{4})\n$$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186129579 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 5 {x}_{1} ( t ) - 7 {x}_{2} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 2 {x}_{2} ( t ) .\\end{array} \\right.$" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 5 {x}_{1} ( t ) - 7 {x}_{2} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 2 {x}_{2} ( t ) .\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129580 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129581 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) &={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) &={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) &={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) &={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129582 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4(0)}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 5{c}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=2.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=1.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4(0)}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 5{c}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=2.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=1.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129583 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} &=9 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=15\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=5$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} &=9 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=15\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=5$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129586 :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, then what is the matrix $A$?\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) - 4 {x}_{1} ( t ) - 9 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) - 7 {x}_{1} ( t ) + 5 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 0\\end{array} \\right.$" "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, then what is the matrix $A$?\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) - 4 {x}_{1} ( t ) - 9 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) - 7 {x}_{1} ( t ) + 5 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 0\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129589 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) - {x}_{1} ( t ) - 5 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 2 \\ln   ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) + 2 {x}_{2} ( t ) = - {t}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.$\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| $x^{′}(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $x(t) +$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) - {x}_{1} ( t ) - 5 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 2 \\ln   ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) + 2 {x}_{2} ( t ) = - {t}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| $x^{′}(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $x(t) +$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186129602 :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above.\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$, then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above.\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$, then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129608 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the eigenvector\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129609 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129611 :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(e,{e}^{2},-e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot 6{x}^{2})i+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot z)j+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{z}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(e,{e}^{2},-e) & =\\frac{6{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(-e)}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }j+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{e}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{e}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6}{e}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\right)}=-6e.$" "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(e,{e}^{2},-e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot 6{x}^{2})i+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot z)j+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{z}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(e,{e}^{2},-e) & =\\frac{6{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(-e)}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }j+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{e}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{e}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $$\n\\displaystyle n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6}{e}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\right)}=-6e.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129616 :question/problem "${∬}_{|x|+|y|\\le 1}\\,\\,{e}^{x+y}\\,dA=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{|x|+|y|\\le 1}\\,\\,{e}^{x+y}\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129617 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y =-x + 1$, $y =-x$, $y = 2x - 2$, and $y = 2x$.\nExpress $∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y =-x + 1$, $y =-x$, $y = 2x - 2$, and $y = 2x$.\nExpress $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA\n$$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions."] [:db/cas 17592186129620 :question/problem "![](images/23/23e724b9fb4e2d06fad67a0bce75d7ff4555ae1dffdda39442e7dee414ea3c78.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x = y^{3}$, $x = y^{2}$, and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate $∬_(D)5ydA$." "![](images/23/23e724b9fb4e2d06fad67a0bce75d7ff4555ae1dffdda39442e7dee414ea3c78.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x = y^{3}$, $x = y^{2}$, and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)5ydA.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129621 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129622 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}y}\\,\\text{d}A$, where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\}.$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}y}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186129623 :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{e^{2x}}{y}dxdy$." "Evaluate the repeated integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{e^{2x}}{y}dxdy.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129624 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3cy &=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y} &=5 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3cy &=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y} &=5 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$"] [:db/cas 17592186129625 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186129626 :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129627 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [:db/cas 17592186129628 :question/problem "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0}$, then $∬_(D)x\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA =$" "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0}$, then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)x\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129631 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{π/3}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\sin θdrdθ$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{π/3}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\sin θdrdθ\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129633 :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {x}^{2} - 4}} , & x < - 3 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x + 4}} , & x \\ge  - 3\\end{array} \\right.$" "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {x}^{2} - 4}} , & x < - 3 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x + 4}} , & x \\ge  - 3\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129634 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129642 :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S$." "$$\n\\displaystyle B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S$."] [:db/cas 17592186129651 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-2y + 3}f(x, y)dxdy$." "Change the order of integration in the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-2y + 3}f(x, y)dxdy.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129652 :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129653 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129654 :question/problem "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from $R^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & -5\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{2} & 5\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n> *To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $θ = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*" "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from $R^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & -5\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{2} & 5\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n> *To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $θ = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186129658 :question/problem "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129660 :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129660 :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186129661 :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x - 9}} , & x \\ne  9 \\\\ 0 , & x = 9\\end{array} \\right.$" "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x - 9}} , & x \\ne  9 \\\\ 0 , & x = 9\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129663 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129675 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x, y)dydx$." "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x, y)dydx.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129684 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129686 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} - 1 \\\\ -\\textrm{i} \\\\ -2 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-1$ and $λ_{2} =-2 + 3\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?" "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n$$\n\\displaystyle v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} - 1 \\\\ -\\textrm{i} \\\\ -2 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-1$ and $λ_{2} =-2 + 3\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129687 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B$, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A = PBP^{-1}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B$, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A = PBP^{-1}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129700 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}^{1 - x^{2}}f(x, y)dydx$." "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}^{1 - x^{2}}f(x, y)dydx.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129702 :question/problem "$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-2 \\le y \\le 0, 0 \\le z \\le 1}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dzdydx\n$$\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-2 \\le y \\le 0, 0 \\le z \\le 1}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129706 :question/problem "Find $B = A^(―)$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2\\textrm{i} & 1 - 2\\textrm{i} & -\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 - 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i} & \\textrm{i} & -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$. What is the value of $b_{12} + b_{33}$?" "Find $B = A^(―)$, where $$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2\\textrm{i} & 1 - 2\\textrm{i} & -\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 - 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i} & \\textrm{i} & -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ What is the value of $b_{12} + b_{33}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129715 :question/problem "$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy$\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n$R = {(x, y, z): - 2 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le 3, 2 \\le z \\le 4}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy\n$$\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n$R = {(x, y, z): - 2 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le 3, 2 \\le z \\le 4}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129718 :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2 - 5\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2 - 5\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129722 :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0d6d2ed215df7808ebafe50b6676c0502b2aed3bd2d3d8661bd4363155eaa65.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$." "![](images/a0/a0d6d2ed215df7808ebafe50b6676c0502b2aed3bd2d3d8661bd4363155eaa65.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129770 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x) = \\frac{3}{2}\\tan (2x + \\frac{π}{4}) + \\frac{1}{2}$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\frac{3}{2}\\tan (2x + \\frac{π}{4}) + \\frac{1}{2}\n$$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186129782 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129782 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period $T$ is given by $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.$" "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period $T$ is given by $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129824 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x) = 3\\cot (\\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{π}{3})$." "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = 3\\cot (\\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{π}{3}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129825 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x) = \\tan (\\frac{3x}{4} - \\frac{π}{3}) + 2$." "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\tan (\\frac{3x}{4} - \\frac{π}{3}) + 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129843 :question/worked-solution "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $0$ to $1$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $1$ to $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $0$ to $1$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $1$ to $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186129844 :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{2}, 0 \\le y \\le 1}$.\n\n1. $∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dxdy$\n2. $∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dydx$\n3. $∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dydx$" "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{2}, 0 \\le y \\le 1}$.\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dxdy\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dydx\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dydx\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129845 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,1].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186129846 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the value of the double integral ${∬}_{R}y\\sqrt{xy}\\text{ }\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,4]\\times [0,1]?$" "Which of the following gives the value of the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}y\\sqrt{xy}\\text{ }\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[1,4]\\times [0,1]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186129847 :question/problem "Calculate $∬_(R)(1 - 3x^{2} + 2y)dA$, where $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(R)(1 - 3x^{2} + 2y)dA,\n$$ where $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]$."] [:db/cas 17592186129848 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [2,3].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$  where $R=[1,e]\\times [2,3].$"] [:db/cas 17592186129849 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}6x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0, \\\\ {e}^{x+y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}6x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0, \\\\ {e}^{x+y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [:db/cas 17592186129850 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129851 :question/problem "What is $∑_(i = 1)^(3)∑_(j = 2)^(3)(i^{2} + j)$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(3)∑_(j = 2)^(3)(i^{2} + j)\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129852 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $∬_(D)x^{3}ydA$, where\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-2 \\le y \\le 0}$." "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)x^{3}ydA,\n$$ where\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-2 \\le y \\le 0}$."] [:db/cas 17592186129853 :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\n$$" "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129854 :question/problem "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129874 :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n$∬_(C)f(x, y)dA = 7, ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA = 1$,\nfind the value of\n$∬_(C∪D)5f(x, y)dA$." "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(C)f(x, y)dA = 7, ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA = 1,\n$$\nfind the value of\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(C∪D)5f(x, y)dA.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129887 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186130324 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ below. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 4} \\sqrt{x} \\, .$\n\n![](images/e6/e6423b96ce60348e780ffd437207bdbeb456e3aae2888d7525f8c4e680f4ee43.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4,$ the value of $y$ approaches $\\sqrt{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} \\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{4} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the square root function at a point is equal to the value of the square root function at that point, and this is true for any point *that is greater than $0.$* However, for points that are less than or equal to $0,$ the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\sqrt{x}=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{c} & \\text{if }c>0\\\\\\text{DNE} & \\text{if }c\\leq0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhy doesn't the limit exist for $c \\leq 0$?\n\n- For $c < 0,$ the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, c} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because the square root function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative inputs.\n\n- Although $\\sqrt{x}$ is defined at $x=0,$ the left limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^-} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because $\\sqrt{x}$ is undefined left of $x=0.$ Consequently, the overall limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist either." "Consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ below. We will use this graph to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 4} \\sqrt{x} \\, .\n$$\n\n![](images/e6/e6423b96ce60348e780ffd437207bdbeb456e3aae2888d7525f8c4e680f4ee43.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4,$ the value of $y$ approaches $\\sqrt{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} \\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{4} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the square root function at a point is equal to the value of the square root function at that point, and this is true for any point *that is greater than $0.$* However, for points that are less than or equal to $0,$ the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\sqrt{x}=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{c} & \\text{if }c>0\\\\\\text{DNE} & \\text{if }c\\leq0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhy doesn't the limit exist for $c \\leq 0$?\n\n- For $c < 0,$ the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, c} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because the square root function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative inputs.\n\n- Although $\\sqrt{x}$ is defined at $x=0,$ the left limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^-} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because $\\sqrt{x}$ is undefined left of $x=0.$ Consequently, the overall limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist either."] [:db/cas 17592186130328 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1e716fb1b2a70d8ee9b74f13cf81e72bb63a147db5a5637a890bf1166b5e87.png)\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=1$, but can we classify it?\n\nNotice that when we check our continuity conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(1) = 3$, so $f(1)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x) = 2$, so $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x) \\neq f(1)$ $\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the first two conditions passed, yet the third failed. When this happens, we have a **point discontinuity**. We also call this a **removable discontinuity**.\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=c$ if \n\n- $f(c)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x) \\neq f(c)$$\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nIn addition, both $f(c)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ should be finite." "Let's take a look at the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1e716fb1b2a70d8ee9b74f13cf81e72bb63a147db5a5637a890bf1166b5e87.png)\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=1$, but can we classify it?\n\nNotice that when we check our continuity conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(1) = 3$, so $f(1)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x) = 2,\n$$ so $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x) \\neq f(1)$ $\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the first two conditions passed, yet the third failed. When this happens, we have a **point discontinuity**. We also call this a **removable discontinuity**.\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=c$ if \n\n- $f(c)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x) \\neq f(c)$$\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nIn addition, both $f(c)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ should be finite."] [:db/cas 17592186130355 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{3} + 1 & - 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3} - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\nA\n=\n\\underbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{P}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{3} & - 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{C}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}^{-1}\n}_{P^{-1}}.\n$$\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n$$\nA\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\\left( {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right)}_{C}\n\\cdot\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}^{-1}\n$$\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)" "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{3} + 1 & - 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3} - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\nA\n=\n\\underbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{P}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{3} & - 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{C}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}^{-1}\n}_{P^{-1}}.\n$$\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n$$\nA\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\\left( {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right)}_{C}\n\\cdot\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}^{-1}\n$$\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $$\n\\displaystyle \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6\n$$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186130390 :tutorial/content "We can express $\\rho,$ $\\theta,$ and $\\phi$ in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ using the following relations:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nTo help to understand where these formulas come from, note that:\n\n- The formula for $\\rho$ is given by the distance of the point $(x,y,z)$ from the origin.\n\n- Since the angle $\\theta$ corresponds to the same polar angle that we see in plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the relationship between $x,y,$ and $\\theta$ in spherical polar coordinates is the same as for plane polar coordinates.\n\n- The formula for $\\phi$ is simply a rearrangement of $z = \\rho\\cos\\phi.$\n\nLet's again consider our point $P$ with Cartesian coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}, 2),$ and convert the coordinates into spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since  $(x,y) = (1,\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the first quadrant, we can compute the corresponding angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2\\sqrt2, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$" "We can express $\\rho,$ $\\theta,$ and $\\phi$ in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ using the following relations:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nTo help to understand where these formulas come from, note that:\n\n- The formula for $\\rho$ is given by the distance of the point $(x,y,z)$ from the origin.\n\n- Since the angle $\\theta$ corresponds to the same polar angle that we see in plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the relationship between $x,y,$ and $\\theta$ in spherical polar coordinates is the same as for plane polar coordinates.\n\n- The formula for $\\phi$ is simply a rearrangement of $z = \\rho\\cos\\phi.$\n\nLet's again consider our point $P$ with Cartesian coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}, 2),$ and convert the coordinates into spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since  $(x,y) = (1,\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the first quadrant, we can compute the corresponding angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(2\\sqrt2, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186130415 :tutorial/content "Our task is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In this case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to begin.\n\nTo carry out the division, we follow the following step-by-step process.\n\n **Step 1** \n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n **Step 2** \n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}2x^2})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}2x^2}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}2x}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}{({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)}={2x^2+10x}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract ${2x^2+10x}$ from ${2x^2+8x - 5}$ and get ${{-2x-5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3** \n\nWe now repeat the process. The dividend is now ${{\\color{red}{-2x}}-5}.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}-2x})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}-2x}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}$ term we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}({{\\color{blue}{x}}+5})={-2x-10}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract ${{-2x-10}}$ from ${{-2x-5}}$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant ${\\color{red}{5}}.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is $\\color{blue}{2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$" "Our task is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In this case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to begin.\n\nTo carry out the division, we follow the following step-by-step process.\n\n **Step 1** \n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n **Step 2** \n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}2x^2})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}2x^2}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}2x}.\n$$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}{({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)}={2x^2+10x}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract ${2x^2+10x}$ from ${2x^2+8x - 5}$ and get ${{-2x-5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3** \n\nWe now repeat the process. The dividend is now ${{\\color{red}{-2x}}-5}.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}-2x})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}-2x}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}.\n$$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}$ term we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}({{\\color{blue}{x}}+5})={-2x-10}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract ${{-2x-10}}$ from ${{-2x-5}}$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant ${\\color{red}{5}}.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is $\\color{blue}{2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186130447 :tutorial/content "Rational expressions are closed when raised to *integer* powers since these can be reduced to repeated multiplication.\n\nFor example, let $S$ be a rational expression. Then:\n\n- $S^3 = S \\cdot S \\cdot S$ is a rational expression.\n- $S^{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{S^2} = \\dfrac{1}{S \\cdot S}$ is a rational expression provided $S$ is not identical to zero.\n\nHowever, rational expressions are *not* closed when raised to *non-integer* powers.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\nS^{1/2} = \\sqrt{S}\n$$\n\nis *not* rational for all rational $S$ because it contains a square root. For example, if\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nS^{1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt x}\n$$\n\nwhich is not rational.\n\nIt's worth noting that $S^{1/2}$ *is* rational for *some* choices of $S$. For example, if we let\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nS^{3/2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)^{1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhich *is* rational. However, since at least one rational expression exists such that $S^{1/2}$ is not rational, the rational expressions are *not* closed under the square root operation.\n\nIn general, applying exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric operations to a rational expression does not result in a rational expression. Let's see an example." "Rational expressions are closed when raised to *integer* powers since these can be reduced to repeated multiplication.\n\nFor example, let $S$ be a rational expression. Then:\n\n- $S^3 = S \\cdot S \\cdot S$ is a rational expression.\n- $$\n\\displaystyle S^{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{S^2} = \\dfrac{1}{S \\cdot S}\n$$ is a rational expression provided $S$ is not identical to zero.\n\nHowever, rational expressions are *not* closed when raised to *non-integer* powers.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\nS^{1/2} = \\sqrt{S}\n$$\n\nis *not* rational for all rational $S$ because it contains a square root. For example, if\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nS^{1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt x}\n$$\n\nwhich is not rational.\n\nIt's worth noting that $S^{1/2}$ *is* rational for *some* choices of $S$. For example, if we let\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nS^{3/2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)^{1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhich *is* rational. However, since at least one rational expression exists such that $S^{1/2}$ is not rational, the rational expressions are *not* closed under the square root operation.\n\nIn general, applying exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric operations to a rational expression does not result in a rational expression. Let's see an example."] [:db/cas 17592186130479 :tutorial/content "When a collection of masses is distributed in two or more dimensions of space, we must take moments about a fixed **axis.**\n\nIn the following work, we will only consider moments about the coordinate axes. \n\nConsider the collection of point masses below, where the distances along the $x$- and $y$-axes are measured in meters. \n\n![](images/32/32f9c207b835469dba729e3d5745dd1225eed6153aac4517e2c40e61b7edb126.png)\n\nThe center of mass of this two-dimensional system, denoted $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ is the point where the entire mass of the system could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the $x$- and $y$-axes as the original system. \n\nMore precisely, the center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ in the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's compute $\\bar{x},$ the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system. \n\nFirst, we denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of these masses are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -2, \\qquad x_2 = -1, \\qquad x_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $\\boldsymbol y$**-axis** is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6-5+16 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_y}{m} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$" "When a collection of masses is distributed in two or more dimensions of space, we must take moments about a fixed **axis.**\n\nIn the following work, we will only consider moments about the coordinate axes. \n\nConsider the collection of point masses below, where the distances along the $x$- and $y$-axes are measured in meters. \n\n![](images/32/32f9c207b835469dba729e3d5745dd1225eed6153aac4517e2c40e61b7edb126.png)\n\nThe center of mass of this two-dimensional system, denoted $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ is the point where the entire mass of the system could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the $x$- and $y$-axes as the original system. \n\nMore precisely, the center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ in the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's compute $\\bar{x},$ the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system. \n\nFirst, we denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of these masses are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -2, \\qquad x_2 = -1, \\qquad x_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $\\boldsymbol y$**-axis** is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6-5+16 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_y}{m} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133482 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc19dd4415663ab027e0fc386d4c5bc81a28c1bc26d5f6cf2083f4f4228a4dea.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" "![](images/fc/fc19dd4415663ab027e0fc386d4c5bc81a28c1bc26d5f6cf2083f4f4228a4dea.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [:db/cas 17592186133485 :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0817c04ee57a8352f8f56a4c232668d415c1573e37a81729752d65c0fb9e1c1.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 36z^2  \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" "![](images/b0/b0817c04ee57a8352f8f56a4c232668d415c1573e37a81729752d65c0fb9e1c1.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 36z^2  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [:db/cas 17592186133558 :question/worked-solution "First, we move the radicals to separate sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} - \\sqrt{x + 6} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we raise both sides to the power of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x + 5})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1)^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= (\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= x + 7 + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= 2x + 5 - x - 7 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we have an equation with one square root on one side and a linear expression on the other side. To solve this type of equation, we raise both sides to the power of $2$ one more time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\n4(x + 6) &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\n4x + 24 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 8x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 10x + 2x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 10)(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 10,-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we check the obtained values by substituting them into the original equation.\n\n- Checking $x=10$, we get\n$\\sqrt{2(10) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(10) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16} + 1; 5|= ^(?)4 + 1; 5|= 5.✓$\nSo, the value $x=10$ is indeed a solution of the original equation\n- Checking $x=-2$, we get\n$\\sqrt{2(-2) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-2) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{1} = 1 \\mid \\ne 2 + 1 = 3.\\cdot$\nThe value $x=-2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {2x +5} -\\sqrt {x+6} = 1\n$$\n\nhas a single solution $x=10$." "First, we move the radicals to separate sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} - \\sqrt{x + 6} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we raise both sides to the power of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x + 5})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1)^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= (\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= x + 7 + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= 2x + 5 - x - 7 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we have an equation with one square root on one side and a linear expression on the other side. To solve this type of equation, we raise both sides to the power of $2$ one more time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\n4(x + 6) &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\n4x + 24 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 8x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 10x + 2x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 10)(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 10,-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we check the obtained values by substituting them into the original equation.\n\n- Checking $x=10$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{2(10) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(10) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16} + 1; 5|= ^(?)4 + 1; 5|= 5.✓\n$$\nSo, the value $x=10$ is indeed a solution of the original equation\n- Checking $x=-2$, we get\n$\\sqrt{2(-2) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-2) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{1} = 1 \\mid \\ne 2 + 1 = 3.\\cdot$\nThe value $x=-2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {2x +5} -\\sqrt {x+6} = 1\n$$\n\nhas a single solution $x=10$."] [:db/cas 17592186133562 :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133580 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}, \\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{w}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w}).$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}, \\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ and $\\mathbf{w}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186133581 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 8 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ - 10\\end{array} \\right]}, \\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ - 15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ - 12\\end{array} \\right]},$ and $\\mathbf{w}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 5 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b?$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 8 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ - 10\\end{array} \\right]}, \\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ - 15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ - 12\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ and $\\mathbf{w}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 5 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186133587 :question/problem "For the vector $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 6 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}}\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$" "For the vector $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 6 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}}\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133588 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{array} \\right]}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\\right\\},$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$" "Given that $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{array} \\right]}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\\right\\},\n$$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186133589 :question/problem "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if $H=\\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.$" "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133602 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- \\frac{7}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{13}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{10}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- \\frac{7}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{13}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{10}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133603 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 5 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-5)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\} = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 4} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 3} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 1}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{-1}{-4} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 5 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-5)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\} = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 4} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 3} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 1}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{-1}{-4} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133604 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{6\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}_1=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_1\\Vert}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2^2+2^2+(-1)^2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}_2=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_2\\Vert}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4^2+(-2)^2+4^2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]} ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2 \\} = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{3}\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{2}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\dfrac1  3,$ $c=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c| = \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| + \\left|-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| = \\dfrac 5 3.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{6\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{u}_1=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_1\\Vert}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2^2+2^2+(-1)^2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{u}_2=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_2\\Vert}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4^2+(-2)^2+4^2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]} ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2 \\} = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{3}\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{2}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\dfrac1  3,$ $c=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c| = \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| + \\left|-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| = \\dfrac 5 3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133605 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{4}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{0}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = 3.$" "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{4}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{0}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = 3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133606 :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{3}{3}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $b=\\dfrac 3 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $\\dfrac{b}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}=-1.$" "We find an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{3}{3}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $b=\\dfrac 3 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{b}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133607 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{12}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{6} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{2 \\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2.$" "Let $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{12}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{6} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{2 \\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle q_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133632 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{v}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{v}_3={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" "Consider the vector subspace $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.\n$$ Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{v}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{v}_3={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133643 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displa