$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = t^3\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186133648 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0,$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0,$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}.$ What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [:db/cas 17592186133649 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3,$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3,$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [:db/cas 17592186133650 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133651 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186133653 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](images/0e/0e4cf57408f9ca18dc4613317b8b9bc8b3149da0bf273ad03a8a8a48a6e6f454.png)" "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown below, then what is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](images/0e/0e4cf57408f9ca18dc4613317b8b9bc8b3149da0bf273ad03a8a8a48a6e6f454.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186133654 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3.$ What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [:db/cas 17592186133655 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133656 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/f9/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/f9/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186133677 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{10}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_3$ dividing it by $\\|\\mathbf{v}_3\\| = \\sqrt{10}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,d\\,| = \\left|-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right|=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}.$" "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{10}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_3$ dividing it by $\\|\\mathbf{v}_3\\| = \\sqrt{10}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |\\,d\\,| = \\left|-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right|=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133694 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133695 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}\n$$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*"] [:db/cas 17592186133699 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},\n$$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise."] [:db/cas 17592186133700 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}\n$$ as a repeated integral."] [:db/cas 17592186133702 :question/problem "![](images/83/83bd636f3a294c641e171c5a8281aaa7068e1931a06229064314e8f96efd0c3a.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\oint\\li