space{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138900 :question/problem "What is $(3 \\cdot 100) + (2 \\cdot 10) + (9 \\cdot 1) + (3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10}) + (7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{100})$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle (3 \\cdot 100) + (2 \\cdot 10) + (9 \\cdot 1) + (3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10}) + (7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{100})\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138917 :question/problem "What are the missing digits in the following equality?\n\n$( 9 \\times  1 ) + ( 5 \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{100}} ) = \\boxed{\\phantom{0}} \\hspace{0.167em} . \\boxed{\\phantom{0}} \\hspace{0.167em} 5$" "What are the missing digits in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ( 9 \\times  1 ) + ( 5 \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{100}} ) = \\boxed{\\phantom{0}} \\hspace{0.167em} . \\boxed{\\phantom{0}} \\hspace{0.167em} 5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139054 :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{7}} + 4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} = 6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{21}}$" "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{7}} + 4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} = 6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{21}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139073 :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5}} - 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} = 3 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{15}}$" "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5}} - 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} = 3 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{15}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139180 :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} + 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{12}}$" "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} + 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{12}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139181 :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5}} - 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{10}}$" "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5}} - 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{10}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139617 :tutorial/content "We can find a good estimate for the sum of two decimals by first rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find an estimate for the value of $16.6+1.3$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$16.6\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 1.3 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 17 & +\\end{vmatrix}1$\n\nThen, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}17} + {\\color{blue}1} = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16.6+1.3$ is about $18$.\n\nThe exact answer is $17.9$, so our estimate is pretty close." "We can find a good estimate for the sum of two decimals by first rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find an estimate for the value of $16.6+1.3$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 16.6\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 1.3 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 17 & +\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}17} + {\\color{blue}1} = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16.6+1.3$ is about $18$.\n\nThe exact answer is $17.9$, so our estimate is pretty close."] [:db/cas 17592186139618 :tutorial/content "We also use rounding to estimate the difference of two decimals. To illustrate, let's find an estimate of $25.85- 12.32$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$25.85\\begin{vmatrix}- & 12.32 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 26 & -\\end{vmatrix}12$\n\nThen, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}26} - {\\color{blue}12} = 14\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25.85-12.32$ is about $14$." "We also use rounding to estimate the difference of two decimals. To illustrate, let's find an estimate of $25.85- 12.32$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25.85\\begin{vmatrix}- & 12.32 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 26 & -\\end{vmatrix}12\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}26} - {\\color{blue}12} = 14\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25.85-12.32$ is about $14$."] [:db/cas 17592186139670 :tutorial/content "When adding mixed numbers, we sometimes get a result greater than (or equal to) $1$ when adding the fractional parts.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the value of the following sum:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac 2 5 + 3\\,\\dfrac 4 5\n$$\n\nAs before, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions:\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$4+3 = {\\color{red}{7}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$\\dfrac 2 5 + \\dfrac 4 5 = \\dfrac{2+4}{5} = \\dfrac 6 5$.\nThis is an improper fraction because the numerator $(6)$ is larger than the denominator $(5)$. However, we can convert this improper fraction to a mixed number as follows:\n$\\frac{6}{5}|= 6 \\div 5; = 1R1; = 1\\frac{1}{5}$\nNotice that this gives us an additional whole number part.\n\nCombining our results, we get\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac 2 5 + 3\\,\\dfrac 4 5 = {\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1 5}} = 8\\,\\dfrac 1 5\n$$" "When adding mixed numbers, we sometimes get a result greater than (or equal to) $1$ when adding the fractional parts.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the value of the following sum:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac 2 5 + 3\\,\\dfrac 4 5\n$$\n\nAs before, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions:\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$4+3 = {\\color{red}{7}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac 2 5 + \\dfrac 4 5 = \\dfrac{2+4}{5} = \\dfrac 6 5.\n$$\nThis is an improper fraction because the numerator $(6)$ is larger than the denominator $(5)$. However, we can convert this improper fraction to a mixed number as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{5}|= 6 \\div 5; = 1R1; = 1\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\nNotice that this gives us an additional whole number part.\n\nCombining our results, we get\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac 2 5 + 3\\,\\dfrac 4 5 = {\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1 5}} = 8\\,\\dfrac 1 5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139672 :tutorial/content "In some cases, we may be required to simplify the fractional part of the mixed number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's convert ${\\color{blue}4}.\\!{\\color{red}6}$ to a mixed number using the normal methods:\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}4}$.\n- The decimal part has $1$ decimal place. Therefore, we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.6 = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{6}{10}}$\nNotice that we can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$\\frac{6}{10}|= \\frac{6 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = \\frac{3}{5}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n4.6 = 4\\,\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" "In some cases, we may be required to simplify the fractional part of the mixed number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's convert ${\\color{blue}4}.\\!{\\color{red}6}$ to a mixed number using the normal methods:\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}4}$.\n- The decimal part has $1$ decimal place. Therefore, we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.6 = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{6}{10}}$\nNotice that we can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{10}|= \\frac{6 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = \\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n4.6 = 4\\,\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139673 :tutorial/content "When multiplying two numbers, it's often faster and more convenient to estimate the result.\n\nLet's estimate the product $18\\times 63$ by rounding each factor to the nearest ten.\n\n$18\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 63 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 20 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}60$\nWe now need to find the value of $20 \\times 60$, which is much easier!\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 60 & ⟶60\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} = {\\color{red}{12}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}00} = 1,200\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\times 63$ is approximately $1,200$.\n\nThe actual answer is $1,134$, so our estimate is pretty good!" "When multiplying two numbers, it's often faster and more convenient to estimate the result.\n\nLet's estimate the product $18\\times 63$ by rounding each factor to the nearest ten.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 18\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 63 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 20 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}60\n$$\nWe now need to find the value of $20 \\times 60$, which is much easier!\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 60 & ⟶60\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} = {\\color{red}{12}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}00} = 1,200\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\times 63$ is approximately $1,200$.\n\nThe actual answer is $1,134$, so our estimate is pretty good!"] [:db/cas 17592186139674 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, we rounded a three-digit number to the nearest ten, but the resulting multiplication was still a bit tricky. We can make the estimation easier by rounding the factors to different place values.\n\nFor example, let's estimate $684 \\times 38$ by rounding the *first* factor to the nearest *hundred*, and the *second* factor to the nearest *ten*:\n\n$684\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 38 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 700 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40$\nHence, we need to find the value of $700 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}700 & ⟶700 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}28}{\\color{blue}000} = 28,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $684\\times 38$ is approximately $28,000$.\n\nThis estimation is less accurate than if we rounded both numbers to the nearest $10$, but it is much easier to calculate." "In the last few examples, we rounded a three-digit number to the nearest ten, but the resulting multiplication was still a bit tricky. We can make the estimation easier by rounding the factors to different place values.\n\nFor example, let's estimate $684 \\times 38$ by rounding the *first* factor to the nearest *hundred*, and the *second* factor to the nearest *ten*:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 684\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 38 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 700 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $700 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}700 & ⟶700 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}28}{\\color{blue}000} = 28,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $684\\times 38$ is approximately $28,000$.\n\nThis estimation is less accurate than if we rounded both numbers to the nearest $10$, but it is much easier to calculate."] [:db/cas 17592186139676 :tutorial/content "We sometimes need to find an equivalent fraction for the fractional part when expressing a mixed number as a decimal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}\\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{5}}\n$$\n\nNotice the denominator of the fractional part $({\\color{red}{5}})$ is not $10,100$, or $1,000$. However, we can still express this fraction as a decimal, as follows:\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}2}$.\n- The fractional part is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$. We can make a denominator of $10$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$\\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{1\\times 2}{5 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{10}$\nSo, the fractional part can be represented as a decimal with $1$ decimal place:\n$\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{10} =0.{\\color{red}2}$\n\nTherefore, we can express our mixed number as a decimal as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}\\,{\\color{red}{\\dfrac{1}{5}}} = {\\color{blue}{2.}}{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$" "We sometimes need to find an equivalent fraction for the fractional part when expressing a mixed number as a decimal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}\\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{5}}\n$$\n\nNotice the denominator of the fractional part $({\\color{red}{5}})$ is not $10,100$, or $1,000$. However, we can still express this fraction as a decimal, as follows:\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}2}$.\n- The fractional part is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$. We can make a denominator of $10$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{1\\times 2}{5 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{10}\n$$\nSo, the fractional part can be represented as a decimal with $1$ decimal place:\n$\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{10} =0.{\\color{red}2}$\n\nTherefore, we can express our mixed number as a decimal as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}\\,{\\color{red}{\\dfrac{1}{5}}} = {\\color{blue}{2.}}{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$"]]
